{"name":"Cache of Camel","hex":"#d3c296","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cache","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A store, protected and often hidden in some way, of things that may be required in the future, such that they can be retrieved rapidly.\nSuch a store of physical supplies, placed by humans or other animals for practical reasons.","Such a store of physical supplies, placed by humans or other animals for practical reasons."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To place in a cache.","To store data in a cache."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cache"},{"term":"camel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mammalian beast of burden, much used in desert areas, of the genus Camelus.","A light brownish color, like that of a camel (also called camel brown)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a light brown color like that of a camel."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/camel"}],"entries":[{"title":"Camel","blocks":[{"text":"A camel is an even-toed ungulate in the genus Camelus that bears distinctive fatty deposits known as \"humps\" on its back. Camels have long been domesticated and, as livestock, they provide food and textiles. Camels are working animals especially suited to their desert habitat and are a vital means of transport for passengers and cargo. There are three surviving species of camel. The one-humped dromedary makes up 94% of the world's camel population, and the two-humped Bactrian camel makes up 6%. The wild Bactrian camel is a distinct species that is not ancestral to the domestic Bactrian camel, and is now endangered, with around 1,000 individuals and a continuing decline."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/07._Camel_Profile%2C_near_Silverton%2C_NSW%2C_07.07.2007.jpg/330px-07._Camel_Profile%2C_near_Silverton%2C_NSW%2C_07.07.2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camel","wikidata":"Q7375"},{"title":"Camel (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Camel is a color that resembles the color of the hair of a camel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Camelcalf-feeding.jpg/330px-Camelcalf-feeding.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camel_(color)","wikidata":"Q17082553"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Camels_by_country_per_capita.png/500px-Camels_by_country_per_capita.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Camels_by_country_per_capita.png/1280px-Camels_by_country_per_capita.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Camels_by_country_per_capita.png/500px-Camels_by_country_per_capita.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACamels_by_country_per_capita.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/2011_Trampeltier_1528.JPG/500px-2011_Trampeltier_1528.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/2011_Trampeltier_1528.JPG/1280px-2011_Trampeltier_1528.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/2011_Trampeltier_1528.JPG/500px-2011_Trampeltier_1528.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2011_Trampeltier_1528.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Camelus_dromedarius_at_Tierpark_Berlin_%281%29.JPG/500px-Camelus_dromedarius_at_Tierpark_Berlin_%281%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&